

# type(scope)!: imperative summary, lower-case, no full stop (<= 72 chars)
#   types:  feat fix perf refactor revert test docs build ci chore style
#   scopes: cli core facade component host preview twins session replay
#           serve snapshot differential soroban-sign-dialog
#           soroban-sim-vitest web-demo js fixtures release pr-preview
#           readme deps
#   (.github/review-conventions.json holds the list; a scope is optional)
#
# The same structure as the PR description, in miniature. Wrap at 72.
# Every commit, whatever its type, has a body with a Why: paragraph; add
# What changes: and Evidence: where they say something the subject does
# not. A one-line Why: is enough for a small, obvious change.
#
# Why: the problem with the current code, and why this way rather than
# the obvious alternative. Numbers for any size, fee or metering claim.
#
# What changes: what a CLI user, RPC client or integrator sees
# differently, and what deliberately does not change.
#
# Evidence: the command that proves it and its result
# (`cargo test -p soroban-sim-core`, 29 passed; replay: 0 unexplained
# mismatches); a test that fails without this commit.
#
# Trailers, in one final paragraph, one per line, each only when it applies:
#   Refs: #123            (or Closes: #123)
#   Fidelity: the results this can move against the network, and on which
#             host (p29, p30), or "none"
#   BREAKING-CHANGE: what breaks and how to migrate (with "!" in the subject).
#                    Upper case: tools ignore "Breaking-Change:".
#   Fixes: <12-char sha> ("subject")   (git show -s --pretty=reference)
#   Co-authored-by: Name <email>
#
# One logical change per commit, and each commit builds. Order a series
# move -> refactor -> test -> behaviour -> regenerate -> docs; a twin sync
# (scripts/sync-protocol-twins.py) goes in the commit that changes its source.
# Fixups: git commit --fixup=<sha>, then git rebase -i --autosquash.
# Review notes, prompts and "addressed comments" belong in the PR, not here.
# Use it: git config commit.template .gitmessage
